  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1226 PM MDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite shows little moisture over most of the state\nat this point, as a broad ridge sits near southern AZ/NM this\nafternoon. There's just enough surface moisture lingering across\nthe Front Range/I-25 corridor this afternoon, which has led to a\nnarrow corridor of MLCAPE >500 J/kg. Across some of the higher\nelevations of the Front Range, we've already seen some weak\nconvective development, though most of the lower foothills and\nurban corridor remain largely capped.\n\nWith continued light upslope through the rest of the afternoon,\nmost guidance slowly dissolves the CAP evident between 800-650mb\n(per recent ACARS soundings), which should allow for convection to\ngradually shift into the I-25 corridor later this afternoon and\nevening. Any more robust updraft would be capable of producing at\nleast some small hail, but the primary threat would be gusty\noutflow winds with ample DCAPE (>1250 J/kg) available today.\n\nThe upper ridge is expected to strengthen over the desert\nsouthwest on Saturday, with 500mb heights approaching 600 dam.\nThe thermal ridge will largely stay to our west during the day,\nbut we should see temperature warm back into the low to mid 90s\nacross the plains. As subsidence increases and the little\nremaining boundary moisture starting to mix out, only a couple of\nweak showers are expected across the higher Front Range mountains\nduring the afternoon with mostly sunny skies elsewhere.\n\nThe ridge is expected to weaken/flatten a bit by Sunday and\nMonday, but this also allows warmer mid-level temperatures to push\ninto our forecast area. 700mb temperatures reach roughly 20-22C\n(near the edge of model climatology) both days, which should lead\nto high temperatures reaching the upper 90s to low 100s. Guidance\ncontinues to oscillate between what day will be the warmer of the\ntwo - but either way, it will be hot. With overnight lows only\nfalling into the upper 60s/near 70F overnight, we've gone ahead\nand extended the Heat Advisory all the way through Monday evening. \n\nThere should be a day or two of cooler weather next week as a cold\nfront attempts to push into the lower elevations Tuesday morning.\nThere may be enough moisture behind the front for a few scattered\nshowers/storms Tuesday, though widespread precipitation remains\nunlikely. The main question is if cooler weather will manage to \ncontinue into Wednesday, or if we'll simply see a quick return to \nabove normal temperatures. It's possible (probable?) that the NBM \nis still too warm for Wednesday, with highs more likely to end up \nin the mid/upper 80s vs the low 90s in the forecast.\n\nAny cooler weather will quickly be replaced by highs in the\nmid/upper 90s by the latter portion of the upcoming week, as the\nridge begins to restrengthen off to our west. With most of our\nforecast area in zonal or northwesterly flow, there won't be much\nmonsoon moisture to work with through most of next week.",
